natural_abilities:
  - { name: "Sees Every Wavelength", description: "All forms. Visible light, radiation, heat emissions, infrared, ultraviolet, electromagnetic and radio. The phantom sees the invisible, reads radio signals as if it carried a receiver, and detects radiation and most forms of energy, magic included. Range for all of these senses: 2000 feet (610 m)." }
  - { name: "Energy Beams", description: "All forms. 2d6 M.D. at first level, plus 1d6 per additional level of experience. Range 500 feet (152 m). Each beam counts as one melee attack." }
  - { name: "Limited Mind-Reading", description: "All forms. Reads the electrical impulses in organic brains and scans surface thoughts - whatever the person is thinking at that moment. Base chance 50%, plus the I.Q. bonus and 3% per level of experience. Range 20 feet (6.1 m) plus 10 feet (3 m) per level." }
  - { name: "Energy Form", description: "Invulnerable to ALL physical attacks - explosions, bullets, hand to hand, everything. Energy weapons, magic, magical weapons and attacks from supernatural creatures do half damage. It cannot lift or move a physical object and has no physical attributes except P.E. It can turn invisible at will, though see the invisible and thermographic sensors still find it. COMBAT IN THIS FORM: +4 on initiative, +5 to strike, +6 to dodge, and it cannot parry, pull punch or roll with impact. Add +1 to strike at levels four, seven and nine and +1 to dodge at levels three, six and ten. No other bonuses apply." }
  - { name: "Light Generation", description: "Energy form, paid for in P.P.E. A flashlight costs one P.P.E. per minute; a blinding million-candlelight flash costs 30 P.P.E.; 40 P.P.E. per melee round makes an aura of true sunlight, which does sunlight damage to vampires and other creatures of darkness." }
  - { name: "Illusions", description: "Energy form. Illusions on every energy wavelength - not solid, but they fool most optical, thermographic and even radar sensors. An illusion of a chunk of uranium fools a geiger counter until somebody puts a hand through it. The cost is 10 P.P.E. times the illusion''s size in feet, taking its biggest dimension (33 P.P.E. times the size in meters)." }
  - { name: "Shapeshifting", description: "The natural form is a crystalline pattern of energy, but a phantom can take any shape it wants. Humanoids, angel-like beings and cartoon creatures are the common ones." }
  - { name: "Total Energy Conversion", description: "Energy form. For 30 P.P.E. the phantom becomes a light beam and travels at the speed of light for a number of minutes equal to its P.E., extendable by 10 P.P.E. per further P.E. minutes. In that state it can hit a target as though fired from a laser cannon: 1d6x10 M.D. at first level, plus another 1d6x10 at levels three, five, seven, nine, eleven and thirteen. Each use costs the phantom 3d6 of its own M.D.C. and two melee actions, and a phantom driven to zero M.D.C. this way has burned off its pattern and is destroyed." }
  - { name: "Physical Form", description: "Building a solid body takes 1d4 hours of intense concentration; abandoning it and returning to energy takes one melee round. The shell can be any shape, and its attributes and bonuses are fixed once made. Those attributes are supernatural, so its punches and kicks do mega-damage - but the phantom is now an M.D.C. creature affected by any M.D. attack and by ordinary physical limits. COMBAT IN THIS FORM: +1 on initiative, +1 to strike, +1 to parry and dodge, plus any attribute or skill bonuses." }
  - { name: "Horror Factor", description: "9. A number the phantom projects, which the sheet has no field for." }
  - { name: "Average Life Span", description: "800 years." }
  - { name: "Size", description: "Varies with the shape taken. Attributes and M.D.C. stay constant whatever the size." }
side_effects: "AN ENERGY FIELD IS A CAGE. In energy form a phantom can be imprisoned in one: if the field is small enough its pattern is constrained, it is trapped, and it cannot damage the field in any way. Phantom catcher field generators are sold on the black market for 20,000 credits and have a range of 60 feet (18.3 m). It is the one thing that reliably stops a phantom, and it is prose the sheet shows rather than anything the app computes."
skills:
  occ_skills:
    - { name: "Mathematics: Basic", bonus: 20, note: "Printed as Basic Math (+20%)." }
    - { name: "Radio: Basic", bonus: 15, note: "Printed as Basic Radio (+15%)." }
    - { name: "Basic Electronics", bonus: 15, note: "+15% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Navigation: Stellar", bonus: 25, note: "Printed as Navigation: Space (+25%). The catalog files this book''s Navigation - Space of printed 151 as Navigation: Stellar; the row was re-cited onto this book in the skills batch." }
    - { name: "Astronomy", bonus: 15, note: "+15% bonus over base. The catalog also holds Astronomy & Navigation; this race is given plain Astronomy, and its navigation skill separately." }
    - { name: "Astrophysics", bonus: 15, note: "+15% bonus over base." }
  occ_related_skills:
    count: 12
    categories:
      - { name: "Communications", bonus: 10 }
      - "Domestic"
      - { name: "Electrical", bonus: 5 }
      - "Espionage"
      - "Mechanical"
      - "Medical"
      - "Military"
      - "Physical"
      - "Pilot"
      - { name: "Pilot Related", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Rogue", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Science", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Technical", bonus: 10 }
      - "Weapon Proficiencies"
      - "Wilderness"
    note: "Select an additional twelve skills, and the book warns that many of them are only useful in physical form. There is no schedule: twelve at first level is all a phantom gets. HAND TO HAND IS BOUGHT OUT OF THESE TWELVE - Basic costs one pick, Expert two, and Martial Arts or Assassin (evil alignment only) three. The app has no way to price a skill against a pick, so a player wanting hand to hand has to spend the picks by hand. Pilot Related is printed as Any (+5% if applicable) and the condition is not storable."
  secondary_skills:
    count: 6
    note: "Six secondary skills from the same list, without the bonuses in parentheses."
extraction_notes: |
  - Read from the OCR cache of a scan and verified against 200 dpi renders of
    printed 37 and 38, which carry the stat block, all nine natural abilities,
    the bonuses and the whole skill list. The lore begins on printed 36 below
    the Draconid''s skill list and every page was read to the end; the entry
    closes on printed 38 at "Alliances and Allies", with the Spacer O.C.C.
    heading below it.
  - P.S. AND P.P. ARE PRINTED TWICE AND THE SHELL''S NUMBERS ARE THE ONES STORED.
    The book prints "P.S. 0 (4D6), P.P. 0 (4D6)": zero in energy form, 4d6 for
    the physical shell, and the sheet has one field for each. The shell''s 4d6 is
    stored, because it is the number a player uses whenever a physical attribute
    means anything at all, and the energy form''s absence is stated in the Energy
    Form ability - "no physical attributes except P.E.". Storing 0 would have
    been worse than a compromise: `rollAttribute` in js/dice.js only parses NdM
    forms and FALLS BACK TO 3d6 on anything else, silently, reporting "3d6" as
    the notation it used. Measured this session; see BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT.md F8.
  - THE COMBAT BONUSES ARE PER-FORM AND NONE OF THEM IS STORED. Energy form is
    +4 initiative, +5 strike, +6 dodge with no parry, no pull punch and no roll
    with impact; physical form is +1 initiative, +1 strike, +1 parry and dodge.
    `bonuses` is applied unconditionally and a phantom is always in one form or
    the other, so storing either set would be wrong half the time - and the two
    differ by five points of strike. Both are in the Energy Form and Physical
    Form abilities instead. Only the All Forms line, "+1 save vs magic and
    psionics, +4 to save vs horror factor", is unconditional and it is what
    `bonuses` holds. Same reasoning as the Silhouette''s shadow bonuses.
  - NO PSIONICS, STATED OUTRIGHT: "Psionics and Psionic Powers: None". That is
    `psionics_allowed: false`, which is modelled - `rollsForPsionics()` reads it
    and the wizard''s Race briefing shows it - and is not the same as having no
    psionics block, which would let the character roll on the Random Psionics
    Table. The Limited Mind-Reading ability is a racial power, not a psionic
    one, and the book files it under Natural Abilities.
  - A RACE CARRIES NO xp_table. Printed 183 heads the column "Machine People &
    Phantom / Vacuum Wasps"; composition is race-primary, so a table here would
    win over the occupation''s and silently drop it. The ladder is: 0, 2,301,
    4,501, 10,001, 20,001, 30,001, 42,001, 65,001, 85,001, 110,001, 160,001,
    210,001, 285,001, 370,001, 450,001. Read off a 250 dpi render of printed 183
    this session; it agrees band for band with the copy the Machine People
    recorded in PR #411.
  - M.D.C. IS FORM-DEPENDENT. 3d4x10 as an energy being, plus another 2d6x10
    while the shell is worn. `mdc_base` is free text and carries both as
    printed; nothing adds them up, and nothing subtracts the shell''s share when
    the phantom drops it.
  - A natural M.D.C. creature, so there is no S.D.C. and no CORE_SDC_BY_CLASS
    entry.
  - The race states no Money, no Cybernetics and no standard equipment; all come
    from the O.C.C. if one is taken.
---

## Lore

A phantom is a mind and a form encoded in a pattern of light and electromagnetic
energy. They are mistaken for psychic entities and ghosts almost everywhere they
go and they are neither. They are one of the very few races that evolved in open
space, feeding on radiation and solar energy, and most of them carry an alien
culture no humanoid can hope to follow.

Not all of them are unknowable, though. A great many become stowaways on
spaceships. One of a phantom''s powers is reading the electromagnetic patterns of
machines and of living things, which amounts to a limited kind of mind-reading,
and the ones who spend time around other sentient beings learn an enormous
amount about their cultures - and often come to like them.

## Tricksters

The result is that a large proportion of phantoms have taken to imitating
humanoid shapes and behaviour, some to be accepted and loved, some to play
tricks on people who cannot tell. They are legendary as shapeshifters and
tricksters and have been the subject of stories from comedy to tragedy for
thousands of years. Over the millennia they have built several strange cultures
of their own out of borrowed pieces and their own peculiar cast of mind.

They are not an evil race. They resemble the faerie folk in loving a good time,
sometimes at somebody else''s expense, but they are markedly more considerate of
other life forms than faeries are. Many form deep friendships and romances with
corporeal beings, and it is not unheard of for a phantom to scatter its own
pattern - suicide - after the death of a humanoid friend or lover.

Some are consummate clowns who adopt a cartoon shape and do silly tricks with
it. Others become dedicated champions of justice. Serious phantoms exist;
humourless ones are the exception.

## Two states

A phantom''s true energy form can take any shape it likes, but its natural one is
a geometric configuration of lights flashing through different colours. Among
humanoids most adopt a humanoid look.

It can also build a solid body and live in it indefinitely. Inside that shell it
deals with corporeal beings normally - and gives up most of its powers and all
of its natural invulnerabilities to do it.

The largest concentration of phantoms in the Three Galaxies is on Phase World
itself, where the race gets on very well with the prometheans and the draconids
and forms an important part of the planet''s defenses.

## Allies

Phantoms are on good terms with prometheans and draconids. The silhouette race
hates and fears them, because a phantom can destroy a shadow simply by putting
on a dazzling enough lightshow.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'phantom');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'phantom';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-phantom-class.sql');
